            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The boundary between the earthly realm and God's heavenly throne, symbolizing divine sovereign order, protection from chaos, and the majestic declaration of God's glory.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6-8\",\"Psalm 19:1\",\"Ezekiel 1:22-26\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A reflection of Ancient Near Eastern cosmology representing a solid dome holding back cosmic oceans, interpreted as a symbol of God conquering chaotic waters to establish structural order.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6-8\",\"Job 37:18\",\"Psalm 104:2-3\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"An allegorical boundary separating spiritual truths or angelic realms (the waters above) from earthly, carnal matters (the waters below), and sometimes representing the authority and expanse of Scripture.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1:6-8\",\"Psalm 19:1\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While historically subjected to rich patristic allegory, modern interpretations are heavily divided between viewing the firmament as an ancient cosmological structure symbolizing order over chaos, a literal description of the earth's atmosphere, or a poetic metaphor for God's transcendent glory.\"}",
